# American English: only the lines that differ from English. The rest is English.

settings-help-topics-welcome-p6 = For a button that could make you lose something, such as a trash can, you need to click twice. The first click fills it with the highlight color and shows a check mark. Click again to go ahead. If you leave it, the button goes back to normal after three seconds.

settings-help-topics-ring-keywords = launcher tray icons brightness tools apps applications start menu open program edge click favorites favorites all apps categories office games type search find filter

settings-help-topics-ring-p3 = Click the sound, Bluetooth, Wi-Fi or battery icon in the middle and a round panel opens over the favorites. There you can change the volume or the microphone, connect Bluetooth headphones, join a Wi-Fi network, or see how long the battery will last and how worn it is. The gear in the panel opens the full Settings page. The cross closes the panel.

settings-help-topics-ring-p4 = Your favorite apps are arranged round the middle. Folders and Drives are among them to begin with. There are also four tool circles that look like apps: Screenshot (the camera), Clipboard, Notifications and the Magnifier. Click an app to open it, or to bring its window to the front. Programs that are open appear in the ring of open windows round the favorites, not among the favorites themselves.

settings-help-topics-ring-step7-1 = Click All apps in the middle. Every app that isn't already a favorite appears round the outside, in sections by type: Internet, Office, Graphics, Sound and video, Games and so on. A type with no apps left has no section.

settings-help-topics-favourites-title = Keeping your favorite apps in the ring

settings-help-topics-favourites-keywords = favorite favorites favorite favorites keep add remove right-click drag move order

settings-help-topics-favourites-p1 = Right-click any app, in the ring or in All apps, and choose Add to favorites. It then always stays round the middle of the ring. To take one away, right-click it and choose Remove from favorites. The middle has room for twelve.

settings-help-topics-favourites-p3 = To change the order of your favorites, drag an app onto another app's place. The other apps move round to make room.

settings-help-topics-switcher-keywords = windows switch switcher alt tab open ring desktop show minimize clipboard copy paste history keyboard on screen

settings-help-topics-switcher-p1 = Open the ring and you'll see your open windows in a ring round the favorites. Each window has its own part, with its program's icon and name. Point at one and the middle shows a picture of that window. Click it to bring that window to the front, or right-click it for minimize, maximize and close. Pressing Alt and Tab also brings each open window to the front in turn.

settings-help-topics-switcher-p3 = The clipboard is one of the four tool circles among the favorites in the ring. It opens as a round panel and shows what you've copied lately, newest first. Type in the filter to find an item, click one to copy it again, and pin the ones you want to keep for good. The small cross beside a line removes just that one, which is handy for a password you copied by mistake. The trash can at the bottom removes everything except the pinned ones. Click it twice to confirm. Choosing a line only puts it back on the clipboard. You paste it yourself, so nothing lands in a box you didn't choose. In Settings, Ring, Clipboard, you choose when the rest is cleared: each restart, after a day, a week or two weeks, or never.

settings-help-topics-notifications-p3 = Beside each line are two small buttons. The pin keeps that notification for good: it moves above the rest, and clearing everything leaves it alone. The small trash can removes just that one. At the bottom are a bell button, described below, and a trash can that removes everything except the pinned ones. Click that trash can twice to confirm.

settings-help-topics-apps-step4-1 = To remove an app, find it in Apps and press the trash can twice. The first press fills it with the highlight color and shows a check mark. The second press removes the app.

settings-help-topics-apps-p8 = The small round key button at the far end of the Search GitHub card opens a box for a GitHub key. You almost certainly don't need one. Without a key, GitHub answers 60 requests an hour from your computer. With one, it answers 5,000, which only matters if you search a great deal. Trindle remembers GitHub's answers for a few hours, so searching for the same thing again doesn't use up any requests. If you reach the limit, the page tells you when GitHub will answer again. The key lets Trindle look at public files and nothing else. It gives no access to your account, and you don't check any permissions when you make it.

settings-help-topics-sources-p2 = A source can install updates for the whole computer, so only add makers you trust. The trash can removes the program and its source together.

settings-help-topics-eye-dropper-title = Picking a color from the screen

settings-help-topics-eye-dropper-keywords = eye dropper eyedropper color color pick picker hex code copy swatch screen

settings-help-topics-eye-dropper-p1 = The eye dropper reads the color of any single dot on the screen and copies its code, such as #FBC711, so you can paste it into a document or a design.

settings-help-topics-eye-dropper-tip3 = Colors taken earlier stay in the list, so you can copy one again later without picking it again.

settings-help-topics-screen-reader-p1 = No screen reader is installed, so the Read the screen aloud switch in Settings, Appearance, under Reading the screen, is grayed out, with a note saying so.

settings-help-topics-look-title = Colors, icons and text

settings-help-topics-look-keywords = color colors color colors accent theme dark light mode sunshine bright font text icons cursor pointer look

settings-help-topics-look-p2 = The highlight color is used for buttons, switches and to show where you are. Pick one of the twenty colors or type your own.

settings-help-topics-look-p4 = Under Icons, choose Circles or Squares. Both are the Trindle Icons: each shows the app's own mark in white on its own color, round or on a rounded square, and looks the same in light and dark. Trindle starts with circles. If the set doesn't have an icon for an app yet, the app's own picture is shown on a white circle or square, so it still fits in.

settings-help-topics-theme-files-p1 = A theme is a whole look, including colors, corners, sizes and spacing, kept in one file. In Settings, Appearance, Theme, every theme is shown as a small picture drawn from its own colors. Trindle's own look, a deep night blue, is always first in the list and can never be removed. Midnight, which comes with Trindle, is black and gray with no blue in it. That is how Trindle's own look used to be.

settings-help-topics-theme-files-p4 = A theme with both looks keeps them in the one file. Its dark values are written in :root { "{" } { "}" } as usual, and its light ones in a second block, @media (prefers-color-scheme: light) { "{" } :root { "{" } { "}" } { "}" }, exactly as Trindle's own sheet does. Give the light block every color and shadow the dark look sets, or the dark ones show through in the light look. Settings then shows Dark and Light beside the theme's picture. A theme that says dark or light has only that look, is always drawn in it, and has no such buttons. If a theme doesn't say, Trindle treats it as having both looks when it has a light block, and as dark when it has none. Other programs are told the look your theme is really drawn in, so a light theme makes them light.

settings-help-topics-theme-files-p5 = A theme is safe to try, whoever made it. Trindle only reads it as a list of values and never runs it, and it can't import another file or reach the internet. Trindle only takes values it knows, and keeps each within its limits. So no theme can hide a button, a question or a warning, or make text too small to read. Words stay clear enough to read whatever colors a theme gives, red stays red, and your highlight color stays your own. A theme changes only your own windows. The login screen always uses Trindle's own look.

settings-help-topics-theming-keywords = theme theming style sheet stylesheet css colors colors shadows corners rounder bigger text size buttons background page store look custom own retheme

settings-help-topics-theming-p1 = Everything Trindle draws comes from one style sheet, written the way web pages are styled. That includes colors, shadows, corners, the space between things, the size of buttons, and the size and weight of words. You, or an AI you ask, can change any of it with a sheet of your own.

settings-help-topics-theming-p4 = Nothing you write can break a window. Trindle ignores names it doesn't know, replaces a value it can't read with its own, and keeps sizes within their limits. Colors are up to you, and Trindle keeps them exactly as you wrote them. It changes its own words so you can still read them on your pages, cards and title bars. A color set for the light look only applies in the light look. A see-through color is checked over both black and white, because Trindle can't know what will show through it. If no color of words can be read on all of yours, Trindle uses black or white. Three things always stay readable: red words about a failure, words on your highlight color, and password boxes, which always use Trindle's own look. If your colors are too hard to read, Settings, Appearance says "Some colors are hard to read." and leaves them as you wrote them. If words you set yourself become hard to read, delete the file and Trindle's own look comes back at once. The file must be an ordinary text file of at most 256 KB. If it isn't, Trindle doesn't use it, and Settings, Appearance tells you.

settings-help-topics-theming-tip6 = If you set --trindle-highlight in your sheet, it overrides the highlight color chosen in Settings, Appearance.

settings-help-topics-files-p2 = Folders opens with one pane showing your Home folder, with Downloads and Documents pinned on the left. The Single and Dual switch at the top left (or F3) adds a second pane. The second pane shows Recent unless you chose another place in Folders' own settings. Open those settings with the cog at the end of the top bar (or Ctrl and comma). There you can set where each pane starts, whether to show check boxes for selecting files, whether to show file endings, and whether a drive opens as soon as you plug it in. Each pane has its own row of tabs above its list. The top bar only has buttons that change how the folder looks, plus Undo and New folder. Everything you can do with the files you've selected, such as Rename, Cut, Copy, Move to Trash, Delete permanently and Wipe securely, is in the right-click menu, and each one still has its key.

settings-help-topics-files-p3 = In the right-click menu, Open with another app lists the apps that can open the file. Double-click one, or press Enter on it, to open the file with it straight away. The check does the same.

settings-help-topics-files-p6 = Press the space bar on a file to look at it without opening it, and press it again to close the preview. F4 opens a terminal in the folder you're in. Right-click a folder or a file and choose Pin to the ring to keep it round the middle of the ring with your favorite apps, where one click opens it. To unpin it, right-click it there. Right-click pictures to resize or rotate them. In a file's Properties you can give it a small emblem. In a folder's Properties you can share it with other computers on the same local network (on a café or office network, that means everyone else on it). The first time, Folders switches on Samba, which asks for your password, and sharing works once the computer has restarted. In the list view, right-click the column headings to add music, photo and PDF details.

settings-help-topics-appimages-p2 = It's listed on the same page with every other app. To remove it, press its trash can twice. The copy you downloaded stays where it was.

settings-help-topics-power-p5 = To log out, restart or shut down, open the ring and click the power button in the middle. The choices appear in the middle of the ring. Lock and Screensaver happen at once. Sleep, Hibernate, Log out, Restart and Shut down ask twice: the first click fills the button with the highlight color and shows a check mark, so a stray click never ends anything. The cross closes the choices.

settings-help-topics-startup-step2-3 = Switch an app off to stop it starting without taking it off the list. The trash can removes it.

settings-help-topics-keys-keywords = keys keyboard shortcuts super windows key alt tab screenshot print volume brightness lock terminal close maximize screen reader desktop show

settings-help-topics-keys-step2-8 = Super and Down: minimize the window. You can bring it back from the open windows in the ring.

settings-help-topics-about-keywords = about version what is debian compositor wayland smithay rust license license gpl warranty risk who made

settings-help-topics-activity-p3 = Trindle adds together all the parts of a program, so Firefox is one bubble however many tabs it has. Two quiet gray bubbles show the rest: Trindle desktop, which is everything Trindle runs to be the desktop, and Linux and other programs. Trindle's apps, such as Settings and Apps, count as programs, not as part of the desktop.

settings-help-topics-activity-p4 = Down the side, four rings show the memory, the processor, the disk and the swap file. On a computer with no swap file, the last one shows the processor's temperature instead. In the memory ring, Trindle's own share is in the highlight color, and your programs and Linux are in blue.

settings-help-topics-activity-step5-2 = Press the stop button on the card. It fills with the highlight color and shows a check mark.

settings-help-topics-bluetooth-connect-p2 = Press Forget and pair again beside the device, or the trash can to forget it without pairing again. Then put the device into pairing mode, as its instructions say, and press Pair beside it when it shows under devices nearby.
